use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Dragging:
    description:
        Dragging lets the user move something by pressing on it and moving the
        pointer, such as a slider's handle, the divider between two panels, or
        a crop area over an image. Everything that can be dragged can also be
        moved with the keyboard. It belongs to the component with the thing
        being dragged.

    starting:
        A drag starts when the user presses on the thing and moves. Tiny
        movements, such as a shaky click or tap, don't count as a drag. How
        far is far enough is up to the chosen library. It works the same with
        a mouse, a finger, or a pen.

    following:
        Once started, the thing follows the pointer until the user lets go,
        even if the pointer leaves the component or the window. While
        dragging, text on the page isn't selected by accident, and dragging on
        a touch screen doesn't scroll the page.

    limits:
        The component decides where the thing can go: along one line or
        anywhere, within limits, and freely or in steps. It stays within those
        limits however far the pointer goes, and follows again once the
        pointer comes back.

    ending:
        Letting go ends the drag. So does anything that interrupts it, such as
        the platform cancelling the pointer, a menu opening, or the window
        losing focus. The thing stays where it had got to. Whether Escape
        cancels the drag and puts the thing back is up to the chosen library.

    reporting:
        The component is told when a drag starts, continuously as it moves,
        and once when it ends, so it can choose to act only on where the thing
        ended up.

    keyboard:
        The thing can be focused, and the arrow keys move it by one step.
        Holding a modifier, or Page Up and Page Down, moves it by a bigger
        step. Home and End move it to its limits, where it has them. Which
        keys are used beyond the arrows, and the size of each step, is up to
        the component.

    direction:
        Left and Right match what the user sees, so where right-to-left
        languages flip the layout, the keys flip with it.

    position:
        Assistive tools are told what the thing is and where it is, in words
        that make sense, such as "Sidebar, 30 percent".

    nonGoals:
        - Not for dragging items onto something else, such as reordering a
            list or moving an item between lists. That's drag and drop, which
            also needs places to drop and a way to say where things landed
        - Not for files dragged in from outside the app. That's a DropZone
